        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The SpaceX-17 mission launched from Space Launch Complex 40 at the Cape Canaveral Air Force Station, Florida, on May 4, 2019, and docked to the International Space Station (ISS) on May 6. The mission (also known as SpX-17 or CRS-17) was the 17th cargo resupply mission flown by SpaceX under contract to NASA for delivery of cargo to the International Space Station (ISS).<br><br>The uncrewed Dragon capsule delivered nearly 5500 pounds of pressurized and unpressurized cargo including hardware, tools, research investigations, and crew supplies. Pressurized cargo included 1601 pounds of science investigations. Among them was the Micro-14C payload in support of the on-going experiment entitled “Characterizing the Effects of Spaceflight on the Candida albicans Adaptation Response.” Candida albicans is a common pathogenic yeast that can pose a danger to those with suppressed immune systems. In some cases, the infection can become life-threatening. This study will characterize changes in the growth of C. albicans in microgravity and allow insight into what conditions in the space environment influence these changes. <br><br>Prior to undocking, the ISS crew repacked the Dragon for return to Earth. Cargo included no longer needed hardware, research equipment, and supplies as well as trash.<br><br>SpaceX-17 ended on June 3, 2019, with splashdown in the Pacific Ocean near Baja California.
        </div>

